From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 15:51:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] EncodingHelper: decode with v4l2request under the rkmpp type

The rkmpp hardware acceleration type reaches the Rockchip decoder through
MPP, which needs the vendor kernel's MPP service. On a mainline kernel the
same silicon is driven by rkvdec, a V4L2 stateless driver reached through
the request API, and no MPP service exists. The rkmpp hwaccel is still
compiled into FFmpeg there, so it is offered, opens, fails, and the decode
silently falls back to software: measured on an RK3588 (Turing RK1, kernel
7.1.7), a 1080p HEVC transcode with -hwaccel rkmpp costs 50.4 CPU-seconds
against 50.2 for no hwaccel at all.

Prefer the v4l2request hwaccel when the FFmpeg build carries it. That build
is by construction built for a mainline kernel -- the V4L2 request API
decoders are not in FFmpeg upstream -- so its presence is the discriminator,
and where it is absent nothing changes. The same transcode then costs 17.0
CPU-seconds and runs 2.3x faster in wall clock.

Encoding is deliberately untouched. The *_rkmpp encoders drive VEPU through
MPP on both kernels and are the only hardware H.264/HEVC encoders available
on RK3588 -- mainline has no V4L2 encoder driver for it, and h264_v4l2m2m
and hevc_v4l2m2m both report "Could not find a valid device". Keeping this
inside the rkmpp type is what lets one acceleration type name a mainline
decoder and an MPP encoder, which is what the hardware actually is.

The 10-bit path is unchanged and still declines: GetRkmppVidDecoder returns
null for 10-bit without a hardware surface, and v4l2request cannot transfer
NV15 to system memory, so 10-bit content continues to decode in software.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../MediaEncoding/EncodingHelper.cs                 | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/MediaBrowser.Controller/MediaEncoding/EncodingHelper.cs b/MediaBrowser.Controller/MediaEncoding/EncodingHelper.cs
index 9a68889..5731ee1 100644
--- a/MediaBrowser.Controller/MediaEncoding/EncodingHelper.cs
+++ b/MediaBrowser.Controller/MediaEncoding/EncodingHelper.cs
@@ -6595,6 +6595,7 @@ namespace MediaBrowser.Controller.MediaEncoding
             var isQsvSupported = (isLinux || isWindows) && _mediaEncoder.SupportsHwaccel("qsv");
             var isVideotoolboxSupported = isMacOS && _mediaEncoder.SupportsHwaccel("videotoolbox");
             var isRkmppSupported = isLinux && IsRkmppFullSupported();
+            var isV4l2RequestSupported = isLinux && _mediaEncoder.SupportsHwaccel("v4l2request");
             var isCodecAvailable = options.HardwareDecodingCodecs.Contains(videoCodec, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
             var hardwareAccelerationType = options.HardwareAccelerationType;
 
@@ -6731,6 +6732,18 @@ namespace MediaBrowser.Controller.MediaEncoding
                 && isRkmppSupported
                 && isCodecAvailable)
             {
+                // On a mainline kernel the Rockchip video decoder is a V4L2 stateless
+                // driver reached through the request API, and there is no MPP service
+                // for the rkmpp hwaccel to attach to: it is compiled in, fails to open,
+                // and the decode silently falls back to software. A build that carries
+                // the v4l2request hwaccel is built against that kernel, so prefer it.
+                // Encoding is unaffected -- the *_rkmpp encoders drive the VPU through
+                // MPP on both kernels, which is why this stays one acceleration type.
+                if (isV4l2RequestSupported)
+                {
+                    return " -hwaccel v4l2request" + (outputHwSurface ? " -hwaccel_output_format drm_prime -noautorotate" + stripRotationDataArgs : string.Empty);
+                }
+
                 return " -hwaccel rkmpp" + (outputHwSurface ? " -hwaccel_output_format drm_prime -noautorotate" + stripRotationDataArgs : string.Empty);
             }
 
